<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Mycenae</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.) · capital Mycenae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Bronze Age Greek kingdom in the Argolid, whose shaft graves, Lion Gate and tholos tombs gave its name to the Mycenaean civilisation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mycenae occupies a hill in the north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> controlling routes to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a>. Its rise is shown by Grave Circles A and B, used c. 1650 to 1500 BCE, with gold masks and weapons. In the 14th and 13th centuries BCE a palace, Cyclopean walls and great tholos tombs such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treasury-of-atreus">Treasury of Atreus</a> were built.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mycenae was ruled by a wanax from a palace with a central megaron and frescoes, and was probably the most powerful of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> centres including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tiryns">Tiryns</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets record perfumed oil and other goods. Its pottery spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and some scholars link it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ahhiyawa">Ahhiyawa</a> known from Hittite texts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a> dates to c. 1250 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The citadel suffered destructions c. 1200 BCE and the palace system collapsed, though occupation continued until c. 1100 BCE. Later the small town was destroyed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> c. 468 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> made it the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Schliemann</a>'s excavations from 1876 made it famous.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1650 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:26.87%;width:10.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mycenae</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mycenae, Mykenai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1375&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mycenae" aria-label="Kingdom of Mycenae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5248 821 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Mycenae lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5568 1077h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5568 1077h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mycenae. <a href="/atlas?year=-1375&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mycenae">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

